## Problem reports

If problem reports are enabled on this instance, your app can offer to send a report when a download fails or a page is not supported. Nothing is sent unless you choose to send one, and the app shows you the exact contents first. A report includes the page address, your optional comment, and only the extra details you choose to include (such as user agent, referring page or downloader error details). It never includes cookies.

Administrators see the report contents — not which account sent it — and use them to fix failing downloads. Administrators may also share a report (without your account identifier) with the FetchDock project maintainers if their support is needed to resolve the reported issue.

You can view and withdraw your reports at any time. Withdrawing a report permanently and irrevocably deletes it from this server, unless it was already shared with the FetchDock project before withdrawal.

You can check whether problem reports are enabled on this instance at `/.well-known/browser-extension` (`features.failureReports`).

## Integrity of the software

This policy describes what unmodified FetchDock does. The instance regularly checks its application code against the files of the official build{% if config.integrityCheckOnline %} and compares them with the fingerprint (digest) the FetchDock project published for this version{% endif %}. This generated policy is only published while that check passes; otherwise, the operator must provide their own privacy policy.

This is a signal, not proof: it catches accidental changes, but a deliberately modified server could fake it. Anyone can compare the instance's published manifest digest with the one attached to the FetchDock release. The check does not cover the admin web app, the operating system image, or the downloader tools (yt-dlp and gallery-dl update themselves).

## Your rights

You have the right to access your data, to have it corrected or deleted, to restrict its processing, to receive it in a portable format and to object to its processing. Where processing is based on your consent, you can withdraw it at any time without affecting the lawfulness of processing before withdrawal. You can delete your downloads, settings, problem reports and your whole account yourself; contact the operator to exercise your other rights.

You also have the right to complain to a data protection supervisory authority{% if a.supervisoryAuthority is not null %}, such as {{ a.supervisoryAuthority }}{% endif %}.

